import{_ as t,a}from"./chunks/studio_equilibrium_dark.hpBR-1mZ.js";import{_ as s,o,c as i,ao as r}from"./chunks/framework.CfhokeI8.js";const n="/ChemistryLab.jl/dev/assets/studio_species_light.B-VEnqfP.png",h="/ChemistryLab.jl/dev/assets/studio_species_dark.hdz0daXn.png",l="/ChemistryLab.jl/dev/assets/studio_path_light.1_UqUml8.png",d="/ChemistryLab.jl/dev/assets/studio_path_dark.B8v1I3t6.png",c="/ChemistryLab.jl/dev/assets/studio_kinetics_light.BSmVyc5e.png",p="/ChemistryLab.jl/dev/assets/studio_kinetics_dark.BtsEj8Xl.png",v=JSON.parse('{"title":"Using the studio","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"studio/tour.md","filePath":"studio/tour.md","lastUpdated":null}'),u={name:"studio/tour.md"};function m(g,e,f,b,y,w){return o(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="Using-the-studio" tabindex="-1">Using the studio <a class="header-anchor" href="#Using-the-studio" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Using the studio {#Using-the-studio}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The studio shows three panels side by side: on the left, the steps of a calculation in tabs; in the middle, the results; on the right, the Julia script the studio writes as you go. The panels can be resized by dragging their borders. <img class="studio-shot light-only" src="'+t+'" alt="ChemistryLab Studio: the materials of a CEM I paste on the left, its phase assemblage in the middle, the Julia script on the right"><img class="studio-shot dark-only" src="'+a+'" alt="ChemistryLab Studio in the dark theme"></p><p>The header holds the file buttons, the <em>Expert</em> switch, the language (English or French), the theme (as the system, light or dark), the status of the calculations and the <em>Run</em> button.</p><h2 id="The-tabs,-in-order" tabindex="-1">The tabs, in order <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-tabs,-in-order" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The tabs, in order {#The-tabs,-in-order}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Start.</strong> The <a href="/ChemistryLab.jl/dev/studio/library#studio-library">library</a> of published formulations, each loaded with one click, and <em>Open a script</em> for a script you saved.</p><p><strong>System.</strong> Everything the equilibrium is made of:</p><ul><li><p>the thermodynamic database: one shipped with ChemistryLab (Cemdata18 by default), or a file of your own;</p></li><li><p>the phases the paste may form: a published phase list, whose phases appear as chips (× leaves one out, ↺ takes it back), or a list of your own with the solid solutions you choose; the C-S-H gel model is chosen here;</p></li><li><p>the species of the database: search by name, formula or cement notation (<code>C3S</code>, <code>Portlandite</code>, <code>CaCO3</code>), click elements to keep the species made of them only, and add a solid to the system with <strong>+</strong>; a ⚠ marks a species whose data do not cover the temperature of the mix;</p></li><li><p>the activity model of the pore solution;</p></li><li><p>the <strong>diagnosis</strong>, kept up to date as you edit, which reports what <a href="/ChemistryLab.jl/dev/api/chemical_systems#ChemistryLab.diagnose_system-Tuple{ChemicalSystem}"><code>diagnose_system</code></a> finds: a constituent the system cannot hold, a phase declared twice, data read outside their temperature range, the Al-Si hydrogarnets of Cemdata18, missing volumes or enthalpies. Each finding says what it costs and what to do.</p></li></ul><img class="studio-shot light-only" src="'+n+'" alt="The System tab: the phases of the paste as chips, the species of the database, the diagnosis"><img class="studio-shot dark-only" src="'+h+'" alt="The System tab in the dark theme"><p><strong>Materials.</strong> The binder, one card per material: a published composition shipped with ChemistryLab, an oxide analysis of yours (by Bogue&#39;s calculation, or kept as oxides), or a mineral composition of yours. A composition is entered in a table; <em>Paste…</em> reads a block copied from a spreadsheet or a CSV file (tabs, semicolons or commas, a decimal comma accepted), shows how each line was read, and adds the rows; <em>Copy</em> puts the table back on the clipboard; Ctrl-Z undoes the last change. Each constituent can be given a degree of reaction: constant, a logistic law in time, a table of measurements, the law of Parrott and Killoh, or any of these capped.</p><p><strong>Mix.</strong> The water-to-binder ratio and the temperature; in expert mode, the mass of binder and the pressure.</p><p><strong>Calculation.</strong> Three buttons choose what to compute:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Equilibrium</strong>: one equilibrium of the paste, its degrees of reaction read at the age given;</p></li><li><p><strong>Path of equilibria</strong>: equilibria along a parameter, each started from the previous one: the age (hydration), the fraction of a replacing material, CO₂, a salt or any species added, renewals of pure water (leaching) or of an attacking solution, a range of temperatures or of water-to-binder ratios;</p></li><li><p><strong>Kinetics</strong>: rate laws integrated in time. Each constituent given a law reacts as the law says (Parrott and Killoh for the clinker phases, Waller for a fly ash or a silica fume, a first-order law, or a law you write as a Julia expression); the others are at equilibrium, to their degree of reaction, from the start. The run is isothermal or in a semi-adiabatic cell.</p></li></ul><p>A constraint can be put on an equilibrium: a pH held, the activity of a species held (<code>CO2@</code> for a partial pressure of CO₂), saturated curing, or adiabatic conditions. <em>Before running</em> lists what keeps the model from running; <em>Run</em> stays disabled until the list is empty.</p><h2 id="The-results" tabindex="-1">The results <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-results" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The results {#The-results}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The tabs of the middle panel follow the calculation:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Phases</strong>: the mass of each solid for the mass of binder, and the volume fractions, the pore solution and the void the chemical shrinkage leaves included. What has not reacted is drawn in gray. Along a path or in time, the masses and the volumes are stacked areas.</p></li><li><p><strong>Pore solution</strong>: the pH, the ionic strength, the porosity and the bound water, and the dissolved elements in mmol per kg of water, on a log axis.</p></li><li><p><strong>Saturation</strong>: the saturation index of each phase.</p></li><li><p><strong>Porosity</strong>: along a path, the porosity and the bound water.</p></li><li><p><strong>Degrees</strong> and <strong>Heat</strong>: for a kinetic run, the degree of reaction of each constituent in time, the heat released, and the temperature of a semi-adiabatic cell.</p></li><li><p><strong>Certificate</strong>: what the solver proved about the equilibrium.</p></li><li><p><strong>Table</strong>: along a path, one row per point; <em>Copy the table</em> puts it on the clipboard for a spreadsheet.</p></li><li><p><strong>Output</strong>: what the calculation printed, the diagnosis first.</p></li></ul><p>Every plot can be saved as an image from its toolbar. <img class="studio-shot light-only" src="'+l+'" alt="A carbonation path: the phases stacked against the CO2 added"><img class="studio-shot dark-only" src="'+d+'" alt="A carbonation path in the dark theme"></p><h2 id="The-script" tabindex="-1">The script <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-script" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The script {#The-script}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The right panel shows the script of the current calculation, rewritten at each change. It is written for a reader who knows little Julia: seven sections, each saying what it does. The studio runs sections 1 to 5 itself, so the results on the page are those of the script; section 6 prints tables and section 7 draws a figure, for the script alone. <em>Save</em> writes it to a file; <em>Copy</em> puts it on the clipboard.</p><p>The model of the page is embedded at the end of the script, in a comment, so that <em>Open</em> restores every form exactly. A script edited by hand opens with a warning: the forms come from its embedded model, and saving writes a new file rather than overwriting the edited one. <img class="studio-shot light-only" src="'+c+'" alt="A kinetic run: the degrees of reaction of the clinker phases in time"><img class="studio-shot dark-only" src="'+p+'" alt="A kinetic run in the dark theme"></p><h2 id="Stopping-a-calculation" tabindex="-1">Stopping a calculation <a class="header-anchor" href="#Stopping-a-calculation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stopping a calculation {#Stopping-a-calculation}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>While a calculation runs, <em>Stop</em> interrupts it. When the interruption cannot reach it within a few seconds, the compute process is restarted, and the next calculation compiles again. A calculation that would need more memory than the machine can spare is refused before it starts, or stopped when it outgrows it.</p><h2 id="What-the-studio-does-not-do-yet" tabindex="-1">What the studio does not do yet <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-the-studio-does-not-do-yet" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the studio does not do yet {#What-the-studio-does-not-do-yet}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p>Surface complexation, the Donnan layer and the thermogravimetric curves are available in the package but not yet in the studio.</p></li><li><p>A glass constituent (slag, fly ash) cannot yet be given a rate law: give it a degree of reaction instead.</p></li><li><p>Under partial equilibrium, a semi-adiabatic kinetic run may stop before its horizon at a change of phase assemblage; the studio then reports the ages the run reached and says why it stopped.</p></li></ul>',25)])])}const T=s(u,[["render",m]]);export{v as __pageData,T as default};
